1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:03,592
Suppose a model is excellent at recovering

2
00:00:03,612 --> 00:00:05,937
hidden words in medical papers.

3
00:00:06,112 --> 00:00:08,089
Would you expect it to be an excellent

4
00:00:08,109 --> 00:00:10,305
search engine for medical questions?

5
00:00:10,468 --> 00:00:12,535
It sounds plausible: it has learnt the

6
00:00:12,555 --> 00:00:15,370
vocabulary and can use the surrounding

7
00:00:15,390 --> 00:00:16,349
context.

8
00:00:18,762 --> 00:00:21,364
But a prediction task rewards a particular

9
00:00:21,384 --> 00:00:24,109
relationship. Filling a gap in a sentence

10
00:00:24,129 --> 00:00:26,944
is not the same task as placing a

11
00:00:26,964 --> 00:00:29,650
separately encoded question near a useful

12
00:00:29,670 --> 00:00:30,964
answering passage.

13
00:00:31,126 --> 00:00:33,014
Start by examining what the model was

14
00:00:33,034 --> 00:00:34,405
trained to predict.

15
00:00:35,316 --> 00:00:37,503
Use the chapter's sentence: The patient

16
00:00:37,523 --> 00:00:40,119
received heart treatment after the attack.

17
00:00:40,268 --> 00:00:44,255
In our C B O W illustration, hide heart.

18
00:00:44,405 --> 00:00:47,039
A local window gives the model patient,

19
00:00:47,176 --> 00:00:49,616
received, treatment and after.

20
00:00:49,792 --> 00:00:51,589
The target, taken from the original

21
00:00:51,609 --> 00:00:53,431
sentence, is heart.

22
00:00:53,990 --> 00:00:55,973
The neighbouring words supply evidence on

23
00:00:55,993 --> 00:00:58,666
both sides. This row illustrates

24
00:00:58,686 --> 00:01:00,714
continuous bag of words.

25
00:01:00,890 --> 00:01:03,807
Skip-gram uses the opposite direction,

26
00:01:03,944 --> 00:01:05,547
predicting neighbouring words from the

27
00:01:05,567 --> 00:01:08,151
target word. Neither arrangement requires

28
00:01:08,171 --> 00:01:10,856
a person to label the sentence's meaning.

29
00:01:11,766 --> 00:01:12,925
Now hide treatment.

30
00:01:13,035 --> 00:01:15,399
A masked-language objective can use the

31
00:01:15,419 --> 00:01:17,706
words before and after that position to

32
00:01:17,726 --> 00:01:19,265
recover the missing token.

33
00:01:19,427 --> 00:01:22,268
BERT's pretraining chiefly uses this kind

34
00:01:22,288 --> 00:01:24,704
of bidirectional prediction to learn broad

35
00:01:24,724 --> 00:01:25,914
language patterns.

36
00:01:26,449 --> 00:01:28,980
Our word boxes simplify a task that

37
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:30,861
operates on model tokens.

38
00:01:31,036 --> 00:01:33,206
The answer comes from the text itself,

39
00:01:33,252 --> 00:01:35,783
which makes this self-supervised learning.

40
00:01:35,959 --> 00:01:38,091
The available context and the target

41
00:01:38,111 --> 00:01:40,371
define the prediction problem;

42
00:01:40,559 --> 00:01:42,717
self-attention is the mechanism that

43
00:01:42,737 --> 00:01:44,301
combines information.

44
00:01:45,261 --> 00:01:47,534
For next-token prediction, show only The

45
00:01:47,554 --> 00:01:49,660
patient received heart treatment.

46
00:01:49,822 --> 00:01:51,490
The next target is after.

47
00:01:51,639 --> 00:01:54,158
Everything to the right is unavailable to

48
00:01:54,178 --> 00:01:56,310
the model at that prediction step.

49
00:01:56,446 --> 00:01:58,475
Revealing the next token in the input

50
00:01:58,495 --> 00:02:00,021
would give away the answer.

51
00:02:00,569 --> 00:02:04,648
GPT-style training uses preceding context

52
00:02:04,668 --> 00:02:07,200
this way. Historically, bidirectional

53
00:02:07,220 --> 00:02:10,099
encoders often started retrieval models

54
00:02:10,119 --> 00:02:13,075
and decoders often powered generation.

55
00:02:13,251 --> 00:02:14,751
That division has blurred.

56
00:02:14,913 --> 00:02:17,084
An architecture family alone does not

57
00:02:17,104 --> 00:02:19,919
establish a present-day model's task or

58
00:02:19,939 --> 00:02:20,614
role.

59
00:02:21,512 --> 00:02:23,467
Pretraining learns broad language

60
00:02:23,487 --> 00:02:26,894
patterns. Domain pretraining changes the

61
00:02:26,914 --> 00:02:28,956
material behind those patterns.

62
00:02:29,105 --> 00:02:31,134
Retrieval-oriented training rewards

63
00:02:31,154 --> 00:02:33,878
relationships useful for the search task,

64
00:02:34,015 --> 00:02:36,739
such as a question and a relevant passage.

65
00:02:37,289 --> 00:02:40,312
That retrieval signal need not be a human

66
00:02:40,332 --> 00:02:41,433
relevance label.

67
00:02:41,595 --> 00:02:43,186
It can come from human judgments,

68
00:02:43,206 --> 00:02:46,743
behavior, weak labels, synthetic labels or

69
00:02:46,763 --> 00:02:48,559
self-supervised pairs.

70
00:02:48,722 --> 00:02:51,421
Ask what those examples and targets teach

71
00:02:51,441 --> 00:02:54,475
the model to preserve, and how well that

72
00:02:54,495 --> 00:02:56,742
transfers to the searches you care about.

73
00:02:57,648 --> 00:02:59,815
The chapter distinguishes three domain

74
00:02:59,835 --> 00:03:03,849
examples. BioBERT continues pretraining on

75
00:03:03,869 --> 00:03:05,459
biomedical literature.

76
00:03:05,634 --> 00:03:08,694
BiomedBERT starts with biomedical text and

77
00:03:08,714 --> 00:03:11,826
vocabulary. SciBERT learns from scientific

78
00:03:11,846 --> 00:03:14,300
literature. These choices change the

79
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:15,949
language experience behind the

80
00:03:15,969 --> 00:03:17,315
representations.

81
00:03:17,859 --> 00:03:20,280
They do not by themselves specify which

82
00:03:20,300 --> 00:03:23,539
query should rank near which passage.

83
00:03:23,689 --> 00:03:26,040
Generic BERT has no direct requirement

84
00:03:26,060 --> 00:03:28,359
from masked-word prediction to make those

85
00:03:28,379 --> 00:03:30,808
independent embeddings useful for

86
00:03:30,828 --> 00:03:33,024
retrieval. Sentence-BERT changes

87
00:03:33,044 --> 00:03:35,370
architecture and training to make

88
00:03:35,390 --> 00:03:38,063
complete-text representations useful for

89
00:03:38,083 --> 00:03:38,977
comparison.

90
00:03:39,900 --> 00:03:42,203
A product can use a BERT-family model to

91
00:03:42,223 --> 00:03:44,664
interpret a query, to produce vectors for

92
00:03:44,684 --> 00:03:47,048
first-stage retrieval, or to compare

93
00:03:47,068 --> 00:03:49,638
records in an existing shortlist as a

94
00:03:49,658 --> 00:03:52,860
reranker. These positions expose the model

95
00:03:52,880 --> 00:03:55,180
to different inputs and give it different

96
00:03:55,200 --> 00:03:56,545
responsibilities.

97
00:03:57,088 --> 00:03:59,017
A family name therefore leaves the

98
00:03:59,037 --> 00:04:01,684
pipeline open. Knowing that BERT appears

99
00:04:01,704 --> 00:04:03,914
somewhere does not tell you how the whole

100
00:04:03,934 --> 00:04:06,298
collection was searched, what candidates

101
00:04:06,318 --> 00:04:08,527
reached a later stage, or whether the

102
00:04:08,547 --> 00:04:10,383
model was trained for that particular

103
00:04:10,403 --> 00:04:11,568
retrieval task.

104
00:04:12,475 --> 00:04:15,131
Carry two questions out of Chapter Five.

105
00:04:15,293 --> 00:04:18,328
Was this model trained for retrieval, and

106
00:04:18,348 --> 00:04:20,840
what relationship did its targets reward?

107
00:04:21,015 --> 00:04:22,940
Where does it sit in the search pipeline?

108
00:04:23,116 --> 00:04:25,686
Contextualisation, domain knowledge and

109
00:04:25,706 --> 00:04:28,005
useful retrieval are related, but

110
00:04:28,025 --> 00:04:28,933
distinct.

111
00:04:29,481 --> 00:04:31,077
The vectors are calculated.

112
00:04:31,240 --> 00:04:33,783
Their geometry is shaped by training.

113
00:04:33,933 --> 00:04:36,516
Whether that geometry helps a searcher

114
00:04:36,536 --> 00:04:37,598
must be tested.

115
00:04:37,773 --> 00:04:40,627
Chapter Six asks what one vector should

116
00:04:40,647 --> 00:04:43,101
represent and how training can make

117
00:04:43,121 --> 00:04:45,511
comparisons between queries and documents

118
00:04:45,531 --> 00:04:46,232
useful.
